# Clean Worktree, Small Commits, Agent Lineage

## Why this exists

A dirty primary checkout mixes unrelated edits, blocks other agents, and hides blame.
Feature and bugfix streams belong in isolated git worktrees. Checkpoint commits
are how we keep those streams inspectable via `git log` and `git reflog`.

## Hard expectations

1. **Primary checkout stays clean.** After each logical unit of work—and before
   ending a turn where you intentionally changed tracked files—stage and commit.
   Prefer many small commits over one large dump.
2. **Never leave intentional WIP uncommitted** on the shared/main worktree when
   you could finish a coherent commit. Scrap experiments and generator trash
   go under ignored paths (`local/`, `.worktrees/`), not as lingering dirty
   tracked state.
3. **Feature work and non-trivial bug fixes use a git worktree** (see skill
   `agent-worktrees`). Do not pile long-lived work onto `main` in the primary
   tree if a worktree is available.
4. **Name branches and write commit messages for lineage.** Branch names and
   commit bodies must identify which agent/session did the work so history and
   reflog are auditable without chat logs.

## Commit cadence

- One concern per commit (single package of “why”).
- Commit after: a green intermediate step, a finished helper, a conflict
  resolution boundary, or “safe to leave and resume.”
- Avoid mega-commits that mix refactor + feature + formatting + docs.

## Lineage (minimum)

Branch (preferred):

```text
agent/<agent-id>/<yyyyMMdd>-<short-slug>
```

Examples: `agent/cursor-composer/20260804-clean-worktree-rule`,
`agent/codex/20260804-fix-grad-reduce`.

Every commit body ends with trailers (fill what you know):

```text
Agent: <product>/<model-or-role>
Session: <short session or conversation id if available>
Worktree: <repo-relative path, e.g. .worktrees/agent-.../slug>
```

Do not invent secrets or personal data. Prefer stable short ids over full URLs.

## When a user rule says “only commit if asked”

For this repository, **clean worktree policy wins for intentional code changes**:
commit when a logical unit is done. Still never push, force-push, amend shared
history, or skip hooks unless the user explicitly asks. Never commit secrets.

## Workflow pointer

For create/list/finish worktree commands, branch templates, and finish/prune
checklist, read and follow the `agent-worktrees` skill before starting
feature or bugfix implementation.
